<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Russian Provisional Government</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1917">1917</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Russian Provisional Government governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> from the abdication of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a> in March 1917 until the Bolshevik seizure of power in November 1917. Led first by Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgy-lvov">Georgy Lvov</a> and then by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-kerensky">Alexander Kerensky</a>, it granted civil liberties and in September proclaimed Russia a republic, but lost support by continuing the war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and delaying land reform. It was overthrown in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/russian-provisional-government.jpg" alt="Russian Provisional Government" width="320" height="301" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Билибин, Иван Яковлевич. Компьютерная обработка: Лобачев Владимир · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Russian_coa_1917.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The authority of the tsar's government began to disintegrate on 1 November 1916, when Pavel Milyukov attacked the government of Boris Stürmer in the Duma. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/february-revolution">February Revolution</a> two rival institutions, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-duma">State Duma</a> and the Petrograd Soviet, both met in the Tauride Palace and competed for power. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a> abdicated on 2 March (Old Style) and the regency was offered to his brother, Grand Duke Michael, who deferred accepting imperial power until the Constituent Assembly had decided.</p><p>The Provisional Government was formed on 15 March 1917 (New Style) by the Provisional Committee of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-duma">State Duma</a> in cooperation with the Petrograd Soviet. Its task was to organise elections to the Constituent Assembly while keeping essential services running. Its first declaration promised a full amnesty, freedom of speech, press, unions, assemblies and strikes, the abolition of hereditary, religious and national restrictions, and the replacement of the police by a public militia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The government could not take decisive decisions amid factionalism and the breakdown of state structures, and real power lay largely with the Petrograd Soviet, which controlled the army and the railways. On 18 April (Old Style) Milyukov told the Allied governments that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> would continue the war to its glorious conclusion; demonstrations followed, Milyukov and the war minister Guchkov resigned, and six socialist ministers joined a coalition. The June Offensive, begun on 16 June, fell apart within three days amid desertion and mutiny, with thousands of Russian soldiers dead, and was followed by the armed uprising of the July Days. Kerensky became prime minister on 21 July.</p><p>In September General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lavr-kornilov">Lavr Kornilov</a> attempted a military coup. Kerensky branded his advancing troops counter-revolutionaries and armed the Red Guard, which left the army distrustful of him and strengthened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a>. The Russian Republic was proclaimed on 1 September (Old Style) by a decree signed by Kerensky and the justice minister Zarudny. On 24–26 October (Old Style) Red Guard forces occupied most government offices and captured the Winter Palace, where the ministers had held out. Kerensky escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pskov">Pskov</a>, where his attempt to retake the capital was beaten at Pulkovo, and later went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Despite its brief life the government passed liberal legislation: it abolished capital punishment, separated church from state and affirmed civil rights, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> was granted independence and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> gained autonomy. Guchkov said it had only the appearance of authority, the real power lying with the Soviet. Pavel Osinsky described the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a> as socialism by default, a result both of the government's failures and of Bolshevik strength, and Nicholas Riasanovsky held that its worst mistake was not holding elections to the Constituent Assembly soon enough.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Russian Provisional Government” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the February Revolution of 1917 the <b>Russian Provisional Government</b> created the Autonomous Governorate of Estonia, uniting the Estonian-speaking districts of the old Estonian and Livonian governorates, and an elected provincial assembly, the Maapäev, met in Tallinn.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-estonia">Republic of Estonia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the fall of the monarchy in February, the <b>Provisional Government of the Russian Republic</b> lost authority as the war with Germany dragged on, and on 25 October (7 November in the Western calendar) the Bolsheviks under Vladimir Lenin seized power in Petrograd through the Military Revolutionary Committee…</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Russian SFSR · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The new government then adopted the &quot;Worker's Marseillaise&quot;, which served until the Bolsheviks overthrew the <b>Russian Provisional Government</b> in the October Revolution later that year.</blockquote><a href="/songs/god-save-the-tsar">God Save the Tsar!</a></li><li><blockquote>In September 1918 anti-Bolshevik groups meeting at Ufa created an All-<b>Russian Provisional Government</b>, a five-member Directory led by Nikolai Avksentiev.</blockquote><a href="/state/russian-state">Russian State</a></li></ul></section>
